As GitOps adoption grows, many teams hit a wall:
🚫 Broken environments
🚫 Inconsistent deploys
🚫 Security gaps
🚫 “Works on my cluster” bugs

Most of the time, the cause isn’t the tool — it’s the way it’s implemented.

After helping teams scale GitOps using ArgoCD in production environments, here are the 10 most critical best practices I recommend to every engineering org:

🔟 GitOps Practices You Should Be Following:
1. Use the App of Apps Pattern
Break monoliths into logical apps. Teams get autonomy while keeping global control.

2. Automate with Sync Policies + Hooks
Enable auto-sync where safe. Use lifecycle hooks (preSync, postSync) for DB migrations, validation, or checks.

3. Define ArgoCD Projects
Set boundaries for namespaces, clusters, and repos. RBAC isn’t a “nice to have” — it’s a must.

4. Enforce Health Checks
Write custom health checks for complex workloads (like Helm, StatefulSets). Production-ready = observable & healthy.

5. Don’t Hardcode Secrets
Use Sealed Secrets, SOPS, or the ArgoCD Vault Plugin. Auditability and encryption are non-negotiable.

6. Use Sync Waves for Order
Control app rollout order in layered setups. Prevent dependent services from failing mid-deploy.

7. Structure with Base + Overlay (Kustomize)
Separate base infra logic from env-specific overlays. This reduces duplication and promotes clean repo hygiene.

8. Environment Branching
Use Git branches or folders for dev, stage, prod. Promote via PRs, not ad-hoc merges.

9. Pre-Sync Validation with Lua/OPA
Use ArgoCD’s Lua or integrate OPA to reject non-compliant changes before they hit the cluster.

10. Review Every Diff Before Sync
GitOps isn’t fire-and-forget. Review ArgoCD’s live vs desired diffs. Catch drift before it causes damage.

🎯 TL;DR
GitOps isn’t just Git + YAML.
It’s a discipline.
These practices make the difference between demo-grade and production-grade GitOps.

🧠 Whether you’re an SRE, DevOps engineer, or platform lead — this checklist is for teams who treat delivery as a product.
